    "body": "# PR type\n- [x] Bug Fix\n- [ ] New Feature\n- [x] Document Updates\n- [ ] More Models or Datasets Support\n\n# PR information\n\nFixes #10374 \n\nWhen lazy encoding fails before and after a checkpoint, recreating `LazyLLMDataset` resets its shared fallback cursor. Resuming at the correct sampler position can therefore return different replacement rows than uninterrupted training.\n\nDerive bounded, non-repeating fallback candidates from a dataset seed and the requested index, only after the first encoding failure. This removes access-history-dependent selection without changing checkpoint code. Keep strict mode, the total attempt limit, valid-row access and exclusion of the initial failed row. Document the deterministic candidate behavior and add resume/access-order regression tests.\n\nThe same failing index uses the same candidate order across epochs. Different failing indices may still share a replacement. Determinism assumes unchanged dataset order, seed and encoding outcomes; the old replacement sequence is intentionally not preserved.\n\nAI assistance:\n\nCo-authored-by: GPT 6 Astra\n\n## Experiment results\n\n- Checkout validation: `python -m pytest tests/general/test_lazy_dataset_retries.py -q` — 8 passed, 9 subtests passed; `pre-commit run --all-files` — passed.\n- The same class was applied directly to installed Swift 4.4.2 and its tests passed there as well.\n- GPU integration reproduction uses Magnus/Megatron Bridge with Qwen3-0.6B-Base, 2×B200, BF16, 8 steps, global batch 4, seed 42, lazy tokenization, no packing/streaming/shuffle, and zero DataLoader workers. Rows 2 and 20 exceed max_length=256. Compare uninterrupted training with an independent run stopped after the synchronous step-4 checkpoint and resumed in a fresh process.\n- Before (archived 2026-10-14 run): first post-resume fallback changes the step-6 batch; maximum loss difference `0.0024992227554321289`.\n- After (final `2**30` seed-range implementation): all 32 batch pairs and all 16 per-rank scalar-loss byte comparisons match exactly; maximum logged loss difference is 0 across steps 1–8. Both ranks resume at step 4 / consumed_samples 16. No tolerance or fallback-state restoration is used.\n- This is an integration reproduction through Magnus, not a full native Swift CLI or multi-worker validation. Each plot compares uninterrupted/resumed runs within one implementation.\n\n<img width=\"2470\" height=\"1330\" alt=\"loss_before_after\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/ccd521eb-de13-449b-903f-bef7c6b88e62\" />\n\n\nThe plots show all eight steps; steps 1–4 of the interrupted/resumed curve come from the independent interrupted run.\n\n<img width=\"2280\" height=\"1216\" alt=\"sample_mismatch\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/6afdd8c1-7368-444d-8a73-d54f3cbc6981\" />\n\n\n# Checklist\n\n- [x] I have linked the related issue above, if there is one.\n- [x] I have reviewed and understand every changed line, and I can explain how the change works and why it is correct.\n- [x] I have run the relevant tests or reproductions locally, and included the commands and results above (if applicable).\n- [x] I am responsible for the content of this PR, regardless of how it was produced.\n",
